# Class and static methods

A normal method receives the instance as `self`. Two decorators change what a method receives.

```python
class Point:
    def __init__(self, x, y):
        self.x, self.y = x, y

    @classmethod
    def from_text(cls, text):
        x, y = text.split(",")
        return cls(int(x), int(y))

    @staticmethod
    def is_valid(text):
        return text.count(",") == 1

class Pixel(Point):
    pass

p = Pixel.from_text("3,4")
print(type(p).__name__, p.x, p.y)
print(Point.is_valid("1,2"))
```

## classmethod

The method receives the class as `cls`, whether it is called on the class or on an instance. Its main use is alternative constructors such as `from_text`, `from_dict` or the built-in `dict.fromkeys`. Calling `cls(...)` instead of `Point(...)` means a subclass gets an instance of itself, as `Pixel` does here.

## staticmethod

The method receives nothing extra. It is a plain function kept in the class because it belongs there, and it can be called on the class or on an instance. If it has nothing to do with the class, a module-level function is just as good.

